    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that formula seems a bit simplified for what i need. the issue is that our lead times from shenzhen aren't constant—sometimes they're 3 weeks, sometimes they're 6 because of port delays or customs stuff. how do i build a safety stock buffer that actually accounts for that kind of variability in lead time so we aren't constantly playing catch-up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To account for variable lead times, you can use the Economic Quantity with Safety Stock (EQSS) approach, which incorporates lead time variability. This approach estimates the space between lead-time variability and demand variability to determine the safety stock level. You can estimate the lead time coefficient of variation (CV) and use it in a formula that takes into account the standard deviation of demand (σd) and the lead time variability (CVt). \n\nFor example, a common approach is the IIR (Input-Inventory Reorder) model, which can be simplified to: Safety Stock = Demand variability * (t^2 / 2) * (1 / Lead Time CV^2), where t is the average lead time in days. This allows you to account for variable lead times and create a safety stock buffer that will help you avoid stockouts.",
